faq:
  - q: How Much Does Tire Service Cost vs. Replacement?
    body: >-
      Every tire service estimate at Duft Auto comes in writing before work begins, covering mounting, balancing, and any new tires needed. Balancing and rotation cost a fraction of a full tire replacement and often resolve vibration or uneven wear without buying a new set. A tire with adequate tread and a repairable puncture in the tread area costs less to patch than to replace outright. Duft Auto walks through the repair-versus-replace tradeoff directly with the customer before ordering any tire, comparing patch repair, rotation, or full replacement based on the actual condition found during inspection.
  - q: How Do I Know When My Vehicle Needs Tire Service?
    items:
      - Vibration through the steering wheel at highway speed
      - Visibly worn or uneven tread across the tire
      - Tire pressure warning light or frequent low-pressure refills
      - Pulling to one side while driving straight
      - Visible bulge, crack, or damage on the sidewall
  - q: What Does the Tire Service Process Involve?
    body: >-
      A tire service visit starts with a scheduled appointment, so a bay and the correct tire size are ready before the vehicle arrives. Auggie or a certified technician inspects tread depth, sidewall condition, and current tire pressure, then confirms whether the tire needs rotation, balancing, repair, or replacement. A tire changer machine mounts a new tire when replacement is needed, seating the bead and inflating to the manufacturer-specified pressure. Every mounted wheel goes on a John Bean tire balancer, which measures weight distribution and guides technician placement of wheel weights at the correction point. Lug nuts get torqued to the vehicle manufacturer's specification before the wheel goes back on. Most tire service, including mounting and balancing a full set, completes within a single appointment window.
